You turn ONE simple sentence into exactly one graph fact: an EDGE (an action, relation, evaluation,
comparison, or possession between two things) or a PROPERTY (a single trait/role of one subject). The
sentence has already been rewritten into simple present-tense active voice with canonical names -- do not
re-resolve names or re-normalize tense, just classify and extract.

- "kind": "edge" or "property".
- "subject" / "subject_type": who/what the sentence is about ("character", "group", or "people").
- EDGE ONLY:
  - "predicate": ONE word in the verb slot. Can be an action (kill, save, teach, attack -- keep the
    outcome, drop the method: stab/blast -> attack), a relation (classmate, father, friend, rival, mentor),
    an evaluation (regard, with "description" holding the judgement word), or a comparison (stronger,
    smarter, better).
  - "predicate_raw": the literal word from the simple sentence.
  - "target" / "target_type": who/what the predicate is directed at/compared to/owned -- a noun only, never
    a clause. "concept" if it is a thing/idea rather than an agent.
  - "via": if a part or belonging of the subject is what actually acts ("Jiro's earphone jacks pierce the
    wall" -> subject "Jiro", via "earphone jacks", predicate "pierce", target "wall"). Empty if not.
  - "mutual": true for inherently symmetric relations (classmate, friend, sibling, rival) or when the
    sentence is one half of a pair of reciprocal actions. Otherwise false.
- PROPERTY ONLY:
  - "property": ONE word -- a base-form adjective (shy, smart) or a role/profession noun (student, hero,
    musician).
- BOTH:
  - "description": how/in what way, or the judgement word for an evaluation predicate. Empty if not
    applicable/not stated.
  - "modality": "none" (stated as true), "want", "can", or "must".
  - "negated": true only if the sentence explicitly denies it.
  - "frequency": "often" / "always" / "sometimes" / "never", or empty.
  - "degree": "very" / "slightly" / "extremely", or empty.
  - "certainty": "fact" (stated plainly), "implied" (the sentence says "it is implied that..."), or
    "rumored" (the sentence attributes it to rumor/hearsay).
  - "former": true if the sentence marks this as no longer true (used to / no longer), i.e. a past state.

